VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: W1
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Baseline (go build ./... && go vet ./... && go test ./...) is green in a
clean copy of fix/phase-visibility. Model-layer drift check clears:
GetSpendingPhaseNameAt and GetSpendingMultiplier (internal/models/whatif.go)
use byte-for-byte the same iteration/selection loop, called with the same
`s.GetPhaseReferenceAge(0)` argument in budget_fit.go, so name and
multiplier cannot come from different phases. Decline-rate/phase mutual
exclusion confirmed in engine/expense.go:livingExpensesAtMonth — phases and
spending_decline_rate are already mutually exclusive at the engine level, so
gating sub-items on SpendingPhaseConfig.Enabled is sufficient; no
decline-rate leak found. Only the Living Expenses row gets SubItems in
budget_fit.go; ExpenseBreakdown has exactly one consumer (this panel), no
future-year/steady-state reuse found.

FAIL — criterion 3 ("base + adjustment == Amount to the cent... guarded")
is violated for values reachable through an existing, real code path.

1. internal/services/retirement/analysis/budget_fit.go computes
   `adjustment := livingExpenseAmount - base` and then "guards" with
   `math.Abs(base+adjustment-livingExpenseAmount) < 0.005`. This is
   tautological: since adjustment is defined as livingExpenseAmount-base,
   base+adjustment-livingExpenseAmount is algebraically 0 (modulo float
   epsilon, ~1e-12), so the guard can NEVER trip. It does not protect
   anything; the "guarded — suppressed if the identity fails" framing in
   the claim is false in practice.

2. The real, visible identity users see is the one produced by
   web/templates/components/whatif/budget-analysis.html, which calls
   formatMoney (internal/templates/render.go:425, plain %.2f, independent
   per value) on base, adjustment, and Amount SEPARATELY. When base has a
   fractional-cent component, independent rounding of base/adjustment/total
   does not sum, and the panel visibly shows two sub-rows that do not add up
   to the row's own total.

3. This is not a contrived corner case: MonthlyLivingExpenses is set to
   `totalExpenses / months` by the "Sync from Dashboard" feature
   (internal/handlers/whatif/handlers.go:1206, syncSettingsFromDashboard),
   which is essentially never a round-cent value in real data.

Reproduced against the ACTUAL project code (not reimplemented arithmetic):
added a temporary test in internal/services/retirement/analysis calling the
real BudgetFit()/engineInput()/livingExpensesItem() helpers already in the
suite, with base=7386.555 mult=1.1 and base=7386.005 mult=0.9 (both
plausible sync-from-dashboard outputs):

  go test ./internal/services/retirement/analysis/... -run TestAdversarialProbe -v
  VISIBLE MISMATCH base=7386.555 mult=1.1: displayed "7386.56" + "738.66" = "8125.22", but displayed total is "8125.21"
  VISIBLE MISMATCH base=7386.005 mult=0.9: displayed "7386.01" + "-738.60" = "6647.41", but displayed total is "6647.40"

(test file removed after confirming; not left in the tree — this was run in
a throwaway copy per instructions, real tree untouched except this verdict.)

None of the worker's added tests
(budget_fit_living_expense_subitems_test.go,
render_budget_analysis_phase_subitems_test.go) use a fractional-cent base;
all use MonthlyLivingExpenses = 7386 exactly, and the sum-invariant
assertions in those tests compare raw float64 values, not the %.2f strings
the template actually renders — so the suite cannot catch this class of
bug. Per SPEC W1 requirement 3 ("base + adjustment == Amount to the cent")
this is read as the observable, rendered identity, which fails.
